-- The questions the Evidence Act is for, and the one record it hands the Criminal Procedure Code.
--
-- This is the only module that is organised by question and not by the Act's own structure.  Each of the
-- questions below is answered by rules in the modules it imports, each rule tied to its section; nothing
-- here restates a rule.  The questions:
--   1. May evidence be given of this fact?                           (ss 5 to 16, 138, 106)
--   2. Is this statement relevant under the Act?                     (ss 17 to 23, 32, 33, 147, 159)
--   3. How may the contents of this document be proved?              (ss 63 to 68)
--   4. Is the accused asked, or required to answer, this question?   (ss 56, 122(4) to (8))
--   5. Who bears the burden of proving an exception or special fact? (ss 103 to 113)
--   6. What does the Evidence Act conclude for the Criminal Procedure Code?  (the interface record)

§ `Conclusions the Evidence Act supplies to the Criminal Procedure Code`

-- Section 147: the statement is "admitted under section 147" whenever one of the section's routes lets it be put to the
-- witness and proved: the previous written statement proved to contradict (147(1)), the previous oral statement proved (147(2)), the
-- previous statement admissible as evidence of the facts stated (147(3)), or the memory-refreshing document received (147(4), (5)).
@ref "Criminal Procedure Code 2010, s 259(1)(a); Evidence Act 1893, s 147"
GIVEN b IS AN `Evidence Act Fact Bundle`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement is admitted under section 147` IF
        `the previous written statement may be proved to contradict the witness` (b's `previous statement facts`)
    OR  `the previous oral statement may be proved under section 147(2)` (b's `previous statement facts`)
    OR  `the previous statement is admissible as evidence of the facts stated under section 147(3)` (b's `previous statement facts`)
    OR  `the statement in the memory-refreshing document is admissible as evidence of the facts stated under section 147(5)` (b's `previous statement facts`)

-- Question 2: is the statement relevant, so admissible, under the Evidence Act?  A statement may be relevant as an admission against
-- its maker (17 to 23), as a section 32 statement of a person who is dead, cannot be found and the like, as earlier testimony (33), as a
-- former statement to corroborate (159), or because it is proved by virtue of section 147.  The Act's other routes (records, 34 to 41;
-- judgments, 42 to 46) concern other things than statements by persons and are in their own modules.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, ss 17 to 23, 32, 33, 147 and 159; Criminal Procedure Code 2010, s 259(1)(c) and s 268"
GIVEN b IS AN `Evidence Act Fact Bundle`, c IS A `Code Conclusions`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement is made admissible by a provision of the Evidence Act` IF
        `the admission is relevant and may be proved` (b's `admission facts`)
    OR  `the statement is relevant under section 32(1)` (b's `hearsay facts`) c
    OR  `the earlier evidence is relevant under section 33` (b's `former testimony facts`)
    OR  `the former statement may be proved to corroborate the testimony under section 159` (b's `previous statement facts`)
    OR  `the statement is admitted under section 147` b

-- The statement is admissible "as evidence of any fact stated therein" (CPC s 268) by the routes that make the statement evidence of its
-- content: an admission against its maker; a section 32 statement; earlier testimony under section 33; and a previous statement under
-- section 147(3) or (5).  Section 159 and the impeachment of section 157 make a statement evidence of consistency or inconsistency, not
-- of the facts it states, and are not included.
@ref "Criminal Procedure Code 2010, s 268; Evidence Act 1893, ss 17 to 23, 32, 33 and 147(3), (5)"
GIVEN b IS AN `Evidence Act Fact Bundle`, c IS A `Code Conclusions`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement is admissible as evidence of the facts stated under the Evidence Act` IF
        `the admission is relevant and may be proved` (b's `admission facts`)
    OR  `the statement is relevant under section 32(1)` (b's `hearsay facts`) c
    OR  `the earlier evidence is relevant under section 33` (b's `former testimony facts`)
    OR  `the previous statement is admissible as evidence of the facts stated under section 147(3)` (b's `previous statement facts`)
    OR  `the statement in the memory-refreshing document is admissible as evidence of the facts stated under section 147(5)` (b's `previous statement facts`)

@ref "Criminal Procedure Code 2010, ss 259(1), 268, 269(1), 291(5)(a); Evidence Act 1893, ss 32(1)(a), 47, 122(4), 147, 157"
GIVEN b IS AN `Evidence Act Fact Bundle`, c IS A `Code Conclusions`
GIVETH AN `Evidence Act Conclusions`
`the Evidence Act conclusions on an item of evidence` MEANS
    `Evidence Act Conclusions` WITH
        `the statement is admitted under section 147 of the Evidence Act 1893` IS `the statement is admitted under section 147` b
        `the statement is used for impeaching credit in the manner provided in section 157 of the Evidence Act 1893` IS `the credit of the witness may be impeached under section 157` (b's `impeachment and corroboration facts`)
        `the statement is made admissible in criminal proceedings by another provision of the Evidence Act 1893` IS `the statement is made admissible by a provision of the Evidence Act` b c
        `the statement falls within section 32(1)(a) of the Evidence Act 1893` IS `the statement falls within section 32(1)(a)` (b's `hearsay facts`)
        `the statement is admissible as evidence of a fact stated in it under the Evidence Act 1893` IS `the statement is admissible as evidence of the facts stated under the Evidence Act` b c
        `the opinion of an expert is admissible under the Evidence Act 1893` IS `the opinion of an expert is relevant under section 47` (b's `opinion facts`)
        `the accused is entitled by section 122(4) of the Evidence Act 1893 to refuse to answer the question` IS `section 122(4) forbids the question to be put to the accused` (b's `accused cross-examination facts`) c

§ `The principal questions, exported`

@export May evidence be given of a fact in a proceeding to which Parts 1, 2 and 3 of the Evidence Act 1893 apply: it is a fact in issue or a relevant fact, the person is not disentitled by civil procedure law, and any fact that must be proved first has been
GIVEN r IS A `Relevancy Facts` @desc The facts about the fact it is proposed to prove: whether it is in issue, and which of sections 6 to 16 or another provision of the Act it falls within
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `may evidence be given of the fact` IS
    `the court admits the evidence of the fact` r

@export Is a statement relevant, and so admissible, under the Evidence Act 1893, whether as an admission, a statement of a person who is dead or cannot be found, earlier testimony, a previous statement of a witness, or a former statement to corroborate
GIVEN b IS AN `Evidence Act Fact Bundle` @desc The facts about the statement under each route: the admission, hearsay, former testimony and previous-statement records
      c IS A `Code Conclusions` @desc Whether the notice requirements of the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 section 428 are met and whether Criminal Procedure Code sections 265 or 266 apply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is the statement relevant under the Evidence Act` IS
    `the statement is made admissible by a provision of the Evidence Act` b c

@export What the Evidence Act 1893 concludes about an item of evidence that the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 asks of it: admitted under section 147, used to impeach under section 157, within section 32(1)(a), admissible as evidence of the facts stated, an expert's opinion, and the accused's entitlement under section 122(4)
GIVEN b IS AN `Evidence Act Fact Bundle` @desc The facts about the item of evidence under each Evidence Act route
      c IS A `Code Conclusions` @desc What the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 has concluded that the Evidence Act needs
GIVETH AN `Evidence Act Conclusions`
DECIDE `what the Evidence Act concludes for the Criminal Procedure Code` IS
    `the Evidence Act conclusions on an item of evidence` b c

@export How may the contents of a document be proved: whether secondary evidence may be given of it under section 67 of the Evidence Act 1893
GIVEN d IS A `Document Facts` @desc The facts about the document, the original and who holds it, and about the notice to produce it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `may secondary evidence be given of the document` IS
    `secondary evidence may be given of the document` d

@export Must the accused answer, or may the accused be asked, a question in cross-examination about other offences or bad character: whether section 122(4) of the Evidence Act 1893 forbids it
GIVEN a IS AN `Accused Cross-examination Facts` @desc The facts about the question and about the accused's own evidence and conduct in the trial
      c IS A `Code Conclusions` @desc Whether Criminal Procedure Code section 265 or 266 makes the evidence admissible
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is the question forbidden by section 122(4)` IS
    `section 122(4) forbids the question to be put to the accused` a c

@export Does the burden of proving a circumstance lie on the accused under section 107 of the Evidence Act 1893, so that the court presumes its absence
GIVEN b IS A `Burden Facts` @desc The facts about the accused and the circumstance relied on, including whether it is within a general exception, or a special exception or proviso, of the Penal Code 1871 or of the law defining the offence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is the burden on the accused under section 107` IS
    `the burden of proving the circumstance lies on the accused under section 107` b
